@image html logos/excalibur.png

Swift 2 is a <a href="https://swift.dur.ac.uk">SWIFT (SPH With Inter-dependent Fine-grained
Tasking)</a> clone which combines the SWIFT SPH kernels
(tasks) with Peano's particle-in-dual-tree concept.
The integration of the SWIFT kernels and Peano 4 has been made possible by the
EPSRC ExCALIBUR grants Massively Parallel Particle Hydrodynamics
for Engineering and Astrophysics (EP/V001523/1) and PAX-SPH.
Swift 2 is realised as Python layer on top of Peano 4 and brings Peano's core, 
its particle toolsbox and SWIFT together.


## Configure Peano

As Swift 2 is a Peano extension, you first have to 
@ref page_installation "configure and build the core Peano code base",
i.e. its libraries, before you start any work. 
Hereby, it is important that you add the particles toolbox:


          ./configure ... --enable-particles --enable-swift


## Log configuration

Swift 2 uses @ref tarch_logging "the standard logging API of Peano as shipped with the tarch".
By default, the generated code searches for a file swift2.log-filter in the execution directory.
If no such file exists, the code falls back to defaults. 
If all info entries are white-listed, i.e. if the log filter configuration is similar to

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 
info  tarch                                -1     white always-on
info  peano4                               -1     white always-on
info  benchmarks                           -1     white always-on
info  swift2                               -1     white always-on
info  toolbox                              -1     white always-on
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 

you should get outputs similar to       

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 
0:00:00 rank:0  ::step()    info    run Init
00:00:04    rank:0  toolbox::loadbalancing::RecursiveSubdivision::enable()  info    switched load balancing off
00:00:04    rank:0  benchmarks::swift2::noh::vertexdata::hydroPartSet::reduceAndPrintStatistics()   info    #lifts=0, #drops=0, #sieves=0, #lifts(assignments)-into-sieve-set=0, #reassignments=0, #local particles=1600 (#remaining=1600, #incoming=0, #leaving=0), #merged virtual particles=0, #eliminated spatial duplicates=0 (#virtual=0,#local=0)
00:00:04    rank:0  toolbox::loadbalancing::RecursiveSubdivision::updateLoadBalancing() info    load balancing's action none with internal state(state=switched-off,global-cell-count=81,lightest-rank-no=1,lightest-rank-weight=81,round-robin-token=0,number-of-state-updated-without-any-split=2,global-number-of-splits=0,local-number-of-splits=0,global-number-of-trees=2,global-number-of-ranks-with-enabled-lb=0,blacklist={(#0:0/3)},heaviest-local-tree=0 (analysed),heaviest-local-weight=41 (analysed),#local-trees=2,is-local-balancing-bad=0)
00:00:04    rank:0  toolbox::loadbalancing::dumpStatistics()    info    2 tree(s): (#0:41/40)(#1:40/41) total=81/81 (local/remote)
00:00:00    rank:0  ::step()    info    run Sort
00:00:04    rank:0  benchmarks::swift2::noh::vertexdata::hydroPartSet::reduceAndPrintStatistics()   info    #lifts=0, #drops=0, #sieves=0, #lifts(assignments)-into-sieve-set=0, #reassignments=0, #local particles=1600 (#remaining=1600, #incoming=0, #leaving=0), #merged virtual particles=387, #eliminated spatial duplicates=0 (#virtual=0,#local=0)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 

While this information is valuable per se, the code base comes along with
some @ref swift_runtime_analysis "analysis scripts in Python" to visualise
the runtime behaviour. Also, the generic @ref peano_runtime_analysis "Peano analysis scripts"
can visualise aspects of the runtime behaviour.



## Bibliography

Whenever you build a Peano application, you get all the C++ code plus a 
Makefile. 
You also get a Readme file (similar file name to your executable) which 
enlists which algorithmic building blocks you employ.
This Readme file might be your first point of recherche.


The best reference for Swift is

      @inproceedings{10.1145/2929908.2929916,
        author = {Schaller, Matthieu and Gonnet, Pedro and Chalk, Aidan B. G. and Draper, Peter W.},
        title = {SWIFT: Using Task-Based Parallelism, Fully Asynchronous Communication, and Graph Partition-Based Domain Decomposition for Strong Scaling on More than 100,000 Cores},
        year = {2016},
        publisher = {Association for Computing Machinery},
        doi = {10.1145/2929908.2929916},
        booktitle = {Proceedings of the Platform for Advanced Scientific Computing Conference},
        articleno = {2},
        numpages = {10},
        series = {PASC '16}
      }

  
The official pidt paper is



      @article{WEINZIERL201642,
        title = {Two particle-in-grid realisations on spacetrees},
        journal = {Parallel Computing},
        volume = {52},
        pages = {42 - 64},
        year = {2016},
        issn = {0167-8191},
        doi = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.parco.2015.12.007},
        author = {T. Weinzierl and B. Verleye and P. Henri and D. Roose}
      }
      



